{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgzC3VPTTFgEmZR2sfsHa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"`KILN_LEGACY_TZ=1` is a real switch in the code, but I can't tell from the repo why step 4 sets it.\n\n- **What it does:** `kiln/timeparse.py:12` makes `parse()` read timestamps with no timezone as UTC when the variable is `1`. That was kiln's behavior before 2.0. Without the variable, those timestamps are read as local time.\n- **Why SETUP.md sets it:** nothing in the repo says. The line arrived in the initial commit `d274cef`, with no comment and no commit message explaining it.\n- **Whether the tests need it:** it looks like they don't. The only test that uses a naive timestamp is `tests/test_timeparse.py:10`. It parses `\"2026-09-02T08:00:00\"` and checks that `.hour == 8`. That holds whether the timestamp is read as UTC or as local time, because only the timezone label changes, not the hour. The other test uses an explicit `+02:00`. So the flag is plausibly a leftover, but I haven't run the suite with and without it, and my Bash approval is gone, so I can't check that here.\n\nI haven't touched step 4, because I don't know what it was meant to guard against. If you tell me it's obsolete, I'll drop it. Or you could run `python3 -m unittest` without the variable. If that passes, removing it is safe, at least for the tests."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":505,"cache_read_input_tokens":21818,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":505},"output_tokens":454,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"<uuid-1>","uuid":"<uuid-36>","timestamp":"2026-11-13T03:08:37.653Z","request_id":"req_011CgzC3VB43mfB9JyzGuDro"}
